messages:
maya: Quick sanity check — wiring IRSA + KMS for a new service (search-ingest). Plan is to let the pod SA decrypt only within the search namespace. Deploy window tomorrow; want to avoid broad key policy changes.

maya: Terraform snippet I'm about to open in a PR:
```
data "aws_iam_openid_connect_provider" "oidc" { ... }
resource "aws_iam_role" "search_ingest_irsa" {
  name = "ri-search-ingest-irsa-${var.env}"
  assume_role_policy = data.aws_iam_policy_document.irsa.json
}

data "aws_iam_policy_document" "irsa" {
  statement {
    effect = "Allow"
    principals { type = "Federated"; identifiers = [data.aws_iam_openid_connect_provider.oidc.arn] }
    condition { test = "StringEquals"; values = ["system:serviceaccount:search:search-ingest-sa"] }
  }
}

resource "aws_kms_grant" "search_ingest" {
  name              = "search-ingest-grant"
  key_id            = aws_kms_key.data.key_id
  grantee_principal = aws_iam_role.search_ingest_irsa.arn
  operations        = ["Decrypt", "GenerateDataKey"]
  constraints {
    encryption_context_subset = {
      "namespace" = "search"
    }
  }
}
```

maya: Any immediate red flags? I'm trying not to touch the key policy if possible.

kevin: Quick > Use the full `sub` claim format in the condition — it needs to be `system:serviceaccount:search:search-ingest-sa`. Also prefer `AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity` style trust (that's what tf data doc will generate).

kevin: Also fwiw, grants are ephemeral and scoped to the CMK; if you need long-term binding consider adding a key policy statement allowing the role to decrypt with a condition on `kms:EncryptionContext:namespace`.

jen: Two small things: 1) The TF `data.aws_iam_policy_document.irsa` block in your snippet might be missing the `condition` key name + mapping shape — double-check with `terraform fmt`/`validate`. 2) For IRSA, annotate the serviceaccount with `eks.amazonaws.com/role-arn: ${aws_iam_role.search_ingest_irsa.arn}` not the legacy iam.amazonaws.com annotation.

sam: If you want to avoid key-policy churn, we did a pattern earlier: create a narrow key policy that allows a role in the account to use the key, and then enforce namespace scoping via KMS grant with `encryption_context_subset`. Works well with short-lived grants issued at deployment time.

maya: Good — I can open a PR implementing the role + grant. If we still need key policy changes we can gate via a support window.

infra-bot: PR created: https://git.company.redwood/infra/terraform/pulls/1489 — plan OK, linter: passed, backend: ok :white_check_mark:

kevin: If you tag me in the PR I'll do a quick review. Also add a test in the infra-conformance suite that tries decrypt with a pod in a different namespace (should fail).

maya: Will do, thanks :thumbsup:
